-- chem/inertiathm.cub — the count of negative levels is a function of the form. (1) A symmetric matrix on Aⁿ, given by
-- its blocks (`it-sym`: the corner a, the row b and the symmetric matrix C below it, so [[a, bᵀ], [b, C]]), has the
-- form F((u₀, u′), (v₀, v′)) = (a·u₀)·v₀ + (u₀·(b·v′) + v₀·(b·u′)) + C(u′, v′) on the tuples of chem/sylvester.cub
-- (`it-fm`), and that form satisfies `sy-BL`: F(0, x) = 0, F additive and homogeneous in its first argument, F
-- symmetric (`it-bl`), from the ring's laws, each a hypothesis (x·0 = 0, 0·x = 0, x + 0 = x, 0 + x = x, associativity
-- and commutativity of the product, both distributivities, the interchange (p+q)+(r+s) = (p+r)+(q+s)). (2) A
-- diagonalizing witness: a list ws of vectors with F(x, u) = 0 for every later x against every earlier u and
-- F(u, u) ≠ 0, tested by `it-ok` (the same facts chem/collapse.cub's `cl-count` flags on the Gram matrix: off-diagonal
-- entries zero, diagonal nonzero). Its vectors with F(u, u) negative (`it-fn`) and the others (`it-fp`) are the split
-- witness of `sy-inertia`: each family orthogonal, of the right sign, their sizes adding to the witness's
-- (`it-orth-fn`, `it-neg-fn`, `it-orth-fp`, `it-pos-fp`, `it-len-split`). So two diagonalizing witnesses of n vectors of
-- one form have the same count (`it-count-form` for any form with `sy-BL`, `it-count-matrix` for a symmetric matrix's):
-- (3) the count `it-count` of any diagonalizing witness is the form's inertia, well defined. Not here: that `cl-count`
-- of the Gram matrix (lists, indices) returns (`it-count`, `it-ok`) of the witness; Bareiss's witness for every matrix;
-- the laws for the binary rationals.
import "sylvester.cub"

